$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM ((3aS,5S,6aR)-5-((3S,4S)-3-Methoxytetrahydro-2H-pyran-4-ylamino)hexahydro-2H-cyclopenta[b]furan-3a-
yl)(3-(tri fluoromethyl)-7,8-dihydro-1,6-naphthyridin-6(5H)-yl)methanone
(3). A solution of 27 (free base, in 1,2- dichloroethane/dichloromethane
solution, 619.7 g, 1.74 mol), 1,2-dichloroethane/dichloromethane (∼10 L
total), and acetic acid (glacial, 180 mL) was cooled to 16 °C. Solid
Na(OAc)3 BH (463 g, 2.18 mol) was added, and the suspension was stirred
for 5-10 min. A solution of 4 (213 g, 1.63 mol) in 1,2- dichloroethane
(1.75 L) was added over 20 min, and the resulting mixture was stirred at
rt overnight (Note 1). HPLC indicated that 28% of 27 remained. A second
charge of acetic acid (20 mL) and 4 (28 g) in 1,2-dichloroethane (150 mL)
was made, followed by Na(OAc)3 BH (54 g) was added. HPLC indicated that
27 had been reduced to 16%. 1.5 h later, a third charge of 4 (27 g) in
dichloroethane (50 mL) was made. HPLC indicated that 12 was reduced to
12%. Fifteen min later, a fourth charge of 4 (11 g) in dichloroethane (25
mL) was made. After 25 min, 27 was reduced to 10%. Solid Na(OAc)3 BH (25
g) along with acetic acid (20 mL) was added. HPLC 45 min later showed
that 27 was reduced to 9%. Solid Na(OAc)3 BH (25 g) was added, followed
by a fifth charge of 4 (8.00 g) in dichloroethane (20 mL). By this point,
the HPLC showed no appreciable change, so the reaction was worked up. The
reaction was quenched with aqueous NaHCO3 (sat'd., 8 L). Part of the
biphasic mixture was neutralized by the addition of extra solid NaHCO3.
(CAUTION: Initially it fizzed vigorously until pH 8.) The layers were
separated, keeping the organic layer. The remainder of the reaction from
the pot was transferred, the pH was checked and, if necessary,
neutralized with solid NaHCO3 , and the organic layer was taken off. The
aqueous layer was back-extracted with dichloromethane (3 × 1 L), but
these were kept separate from the main extract because this
dichloromethane was enriched in 27. The combined organic layer was washed
with brine (2 L), dried (Na2SO4), filtered, and evaporated under reduced
pressure. The brine layer was back-extracted with dichloromethane, dried
over Na2SO4 with the other back-extracts, and evaporated to give 74.2 g
of off-quality 3 that was repurified later. The main product gave crude
product (884 g) that was diluted with 1.5 L of dichloromethane, divided
into 3 equal portions, and purified according to the general procedure
below: BIOTAGE (5 kg, prewetted with 8 L dichloromethane), liquid load
890 g of dichloromethane solution, eluted with 8 L dichloromethane, 24 L
of 4% MeOH (7N NH3) in dichloromethane, then 40 L of 5.5% MeOH (7N NH3)
in dichloromethane (2 L fractions were taken, and all prospective product
fractions were assayed by HPLC (eclstd). Typically ∼240 g of satisfactory
product was collected. The collected enriched isomer (769 g) was
separated using 65 injections on 2 kg Chiralpak-AS in 11 cm column;
eluent: 90 heptane:9 EtOH:1 MeOH (saturated with ammonia); Flow 500
mL/min; UV 265 nm; 1 recycle and collection; evaporation at 38 °C for ±5
h. There was returned to give product. Yield: 75%, 609 g (with 9 wt %/wt
EtOH). 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) 8.71 (s, 1H), 7.70 (br. s., 1H), 5.05 (d,
J = 4.6 Hz, 1H), 4.85-4.72 (m, 2H), 4.08 (dd, J = 3.2, 12.5 Hz, 1H),
4.02-3.85 (m, 4H), 3.75-3.52 (m, obscured by EtOH), 3.44-3.35 (m, 4H),
3.35-3.25 (m, 2H), 3.21-3.08 (m, 2H), 2.76 (d, J = 10.3 Hz, 1H), 2.34
(br. s., 1H), 2.21 (dd, J = 5.6, 13.2 Hz, 2H), 2.14-1.93 (m, 1H),
1.78-1.50 (m, 8H); [α]D25 -13.7° (c 1) (MeOH), HPLC (betaSD): 5.84 min,
89.4% (uncorrected); Chiral HPLC (standard): 5.54 min, 97.7%.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM incremental addition of Na(OAc)3BH, AcOH, DCE and
(3R)-3-methoxytetrahydro-4H-pyran-4-one in stage 3
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Lab-Scale Preparation of a Novel Cyclopenta[b]furan Chemokine Receptor
